          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between cladding and plaster finishing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Plaster and paint are applied directly and bond to the wall surface, while cladding is installed on a substructure — a frame of metal or timber battens fixed to the wall — which creates an air gap behind the finished surface. That gap allows drainage, ventilation and thermal separation, which is part of why well-installed cladding systems often outperform direct-applied finishes over the long term, particularly under India's combination of intense sun and heavy monsoon rainfall."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which elevation cladding material lasts the longest?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Engineered metal cladding systems, including aluminium and steel panels with a proper PVDF or comparable coating, generally carry the longest formal warranties, typically 15 to 30 years, and are the category most likely to justify their higher upfront cost against total cost of ownership over two to three decades. Brick and stone cladding are also very durable and low maintenance, though they do not carry the same kind of coating warranty."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How does climate affect which cladding material I should choose?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In coastal regions, salt-laden air accelerates corrosion, making marine-grade aluminium with a robust PVDF coating the safer long-term choice. In hot, dry climates, heat-reflective coatings and lighter coloured metal panels perform better than dark, heat-absorbing finishes. In high-rainfall regions, the drainage detailing behind the cladding matters more than the surface material itself, since even a premium finish will fail prematurely if water pools or seeps behind the panel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the most common mistake when installing elevation cladding?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Skipping or under-specifying the drainage gap behind the cladding to save on substructure cost is the most common cause of early failure. Other frequent mistakes include choosing a material based only on an indoor showroom sample without checking outdoor climate performance, and mixing too many cladding materials across one facade, which increases both cost and the number of junctions that need careful waterproofing."
          }
        }
